(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the syntactic mechanics of voice transformation from active to passive across the full spectrum of English tenses.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Core Transformation Formula:) Tj
T*
(   Voice in English syntax denotes the relationship between the event described by the verb and) Tj
T*
(   the participants identified by its arguments:) Tj
T*
(   - Active: [Subject / Agent] + [Finite Verb] + [Direct Object / Patient].) Tj
T*
(   - Passive: [Patient as Grammatical Subject] + [Auxiliary BE in appropriate tense/aspect] +) Tj
T*
(   [Past Participle \(V3\)] + [\(optional\) Prepositional Phrase 'by' + Agent].) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Tense-by-Tense Passive Conjugation Paradigm:) Tj
T*
(   - Simple Present: "The enzyme cleaves the vector." -> "The vector is cleaved by the enzyme.") Tj
T*
(   - Present Continuous: "We are culturing the hybridoma." -> "The hybridoma is being cultured.") Tj
T*
(   - Present Perfect: "The team has synthesized the peptide." -> "The peptide has been) Tj
T*
(   synthesized.") Tj
T*
(   - Simple Past: "The technician centrifuged the lysate." -> "The lysate was centrifuged.") Tj
T*
(   - Past Continuous: "The operator was monitoring the bioreactor." -> "The bioreactor was being) Tj
T*
(   monitored.") Tj
T*
(   - Past Perfect: "They had calibrated the electrode." -> "The electrode had been calibrated.") Tj
T*
(   - Future Simple: "The lab will validate the diagnostic kit." -> "The diagnostic kit will be) Tj
T*
(   validated.") Tj
T*
(   - Modal Auxiliaries: "You must autoclave the media." -> "The media must be autoclaved.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Invariable Linguistic Invariant:) Tj
T*
(   In every passive construction, the main lexical verb appears strictly as a past participle;) Tj
T*
(   tense, number, and aspectual inflection are borne entirely by the auxiliary 'be'.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the rhetorical, scientific, and cognitive justifications for the dominance of the agentless passive in experimental methodologies.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Agentless \(Truncated\) Passive Defined:) Tj
T*
(   A passive construction in which the agent of the action \(the 'by'-phrase\) is omitted:) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "The reaction mixture was incubated at 37 deg C for 48 hours." \(Omits 'by the) Tj
T*
(   experimenters'\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Four Scientific Justifications for Agentless Passivity:) Tj
T*
(   - Epistemological Objectivity and Depersonalization:) Tj
T*
(   - Scientific epistemology demands that experimental protocols be universally reproducible by) Tj
T*
(   any qualified technician worldwide. Inserting personal agents \("I pipetted...", "We) Tj
T*
(   centrifuged..."\) introduces irrelevant subjective idiosyncrasy. The focus must rest entirely) Tj
T*
(   upon the physical transformation of the biological substrate.) Tj
T*
(   - Thematic Prominence \(Topic-Focus / Given-New Flow\):) Tj
T*
(   - The passive moves the experimental material \(the patient\) into the sentence-initial subject) Tj
T*
(   position \(the theme\), aligning it with the reader's cognitive focus:) Tj
T*
(   - Passive: "Genomic DNA was extracted from whole blood..." \(Focuses on DNA\).) Tj
T*
(   - Active: "We extracted genomic DNA..." \(Focuses on human researchers\).) Tj
T*
(   - Informational Economy and Concision:) Tj
T*
(   - Repeating "by the authors" or "by the technician" in every procedural sentence creates) Tj
T*
(   tedious lexical bloat without providing new empirical information.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
