(>> Solution to Question 11:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss the Mandative Subjunctive versus modal periphrastics \('should'\) in international biosafety standards.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Competing Syntactic Strategies for Directives:) Tj
T*
(   In institutional guidelines, biosafety manuals \(CDC/NIH BMBL\), and ISO cleanroom directives,) Tj
T*
(   mandatory compliance can be articulated via two distinct syntactic mechanisms:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The American Mandative Subjunctive:) Tj
T*
(   - Formula: [Matrix Verb] + that + [Subject] + [Bare Base Verb]:) Tj
T*
(   - "The Institutional Biosafety Committee requires that every laboratory technician undergo) Tj
T*
(   blood-borne pathogen certification.") Tj
T*
(   - "The SOP dictates that the laminar airflow hood remain operational 24/7.") Tj
T*
(   - Notice: 'undergo' \(not *undergoes\); 'remain' \(not *remains\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. The British / International Modal Periphrastic \('Should'\):) Tj
T*
(   - Formula: [Matrix Verb] + that + [Subject] + should + [Base Verb]:) Tj
T*
(   - "The Institutional Biosafety Committee requires that every laboratory technician should) Tj
T*
(   undergo blood-borne pathogen certification.") Tj
T*
(   - "The SOP dictates that the hood should remain operational.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Stylistic Recommendations:) Tj
T*
(   Major American scientific journals prefer the concise mandative subjunctive; European and) Tj
T*
(   Commonwealth bodies often utilize the 'should'-periphrastic. Both are fully standard, but) Tj
T*
(   internal consistency within a manuscript is mandatory.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 12:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the misuse of 'can' versus 'may' in scientific laboratory permissions and protocol documentation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Traditional Prescriptive Distinction:) Tj
T*
(   - 'Can': Denotes dynamic physical ability, technical capacity, or objective possibility:) Tj
T*
(   - "The robotic pipettor can dispense volumes down to 0.1 microliters." \(Technical capacity\).) Tj
T*
(   - 'May': Denotes formal deontic permission, authorization, or epistemic possibility:) Tj
T*
(   - "Trained personnel may enter the BSL-3 biocontainment suite." \(Formal authorization\).) Tj
T*
(   - "The mutation may alter active-site stereochemistry." \(Epistemic possibility\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Protocol Ambiguity Hazards:) Tj
T*
(   Confusing 'can' and 'may' in standard operating procedures creates operational hazards:) Tj
T*
(   - Ambiguous SOP: "Technicians can discard used culture plates in standard biological waste.") Tj
T*
(   - Ambiguity: Does this mean they have the physical strength to throw them away, or are they) Tj
T*
(   legally permitted to do so?) Tj
T*
(   - Precise SOP: "Technicians may discard used culture plates in standard biohazard autoclaving) Tj
T*
(   bags upon completing deactivation.") Tj
T*
(   - Rule: Use 'may' for discretionary authorized actions; use 'must' for mandatory directives;) Tj
T*
(   reserve 'can' strictly for physical/instrumental capability.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 13:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine passive transformations of causative verbs \('make', 'let', 'have'\) in experimental methodology descriptions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Causative Verb Syntax in Active Voice:) Tj
T*
(   Causative verbs describe situations where an agent causes another entity to perform an action.) Tj
T*
ET

