ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the epistemic strength of modal verbs in constructing formal scientific hypotheses.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Hypotheses as Probabilistic Propositions:) Tj
T*
(   A scientific hypothesis represents an informed deduction that accounts for empirical) Tj
T*
(   observations while proposing a falsifiable, testable causal mechanism.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Modal Spectrum in Hypothesis Formulation:) Tj
T*
(   - High Epistemic Commitment \(Indicative / Should / Must\):) Tj
T*
(   - "We hypothesize that phosphorylation at Ser-128 is essential for kinase activation." \(Direct) Tj
T*
(   indicative - strongest commitment\).) Tj
T*
(   - "Dimerization must be required for catalytic cleft formation." \(Strong deductive necessity\).) Tj
T*
(   - Moderate Epistemic Commitment \(May / Could\):) Tj
T*
(   - "We hypothesize that the novel cyclic peptide may inhibit viral entry by occluding the spike) Tj
T*
(   glycoprotein receptor-binding domain.") Tj
T*
(   - Stance: Proposes a credible physical model without over-committing prior to structural) Tj
T*
(   validation.) Tj
T*
(   - Tentative Exploratory Hypothesis \(Might\):) Tj
T*
(   - "Aberrant chromatin remodeling might contribute to transcriptional silencing in resistant) Tj
T*
(   subclones.") Tj
T*
(   - Stance: Acknowledges that the proposed pathway is one of many potential hypotheses.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 17:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the passive voice of phrasal verbs, prepositional verbs, and multi-word verbs in biotechnology literature.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Classification of Multi-Word Verbs:) Tj
T*
(   - Phrasal Verbs \(Verb + Adverbial Particle, e.g., carry out, point out, set up\).) Tj
T*
(   - Prepositional Verbs \(Verb + Preposition, e.g., adhere to, account for, cope with\).) Tj
T*
(   - Phrasal-Prepositional Verbs \(Verb + Particle + Preposition, e.g., look forward to, put up) Tj
T*
(   with\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Passivization Rules:) Tj
T*
(   The particle or preposition MUST remain strictly attached to the lexical verb; it cannot be) Tj
T*
(   discarded or relocated:) Tj
T*
(   - Active: "The laboratory carried out the clinical screening.") Tj
T*
(   - Passive: "The clinical screening was carried out by the laboratory." \(Never *was carried\).) Tj
T*
(   - Active: "Researchers have done away with radioactive phosphorus labelling.") Tj
T*
(   - Passive: "Radioactive phosphorus labelling has been done away with.") Tj
T*
(   - Active: "The bioengineers came up with an automated feed schedule.") Tj
T*
(   - Passive: "An automated feed schedule was come up with." \(Note: in formal style, replace with) Tj
T*
(   single-word Latinate verb: "An automated feed schedule was devised"\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
